## The update loop

Every tick, every keypress, every Docker event becomes a `Msg`. Commands return the next message, so async work never touches shared state directly.

```go
func (m model) Update(msg tea.Msg) (tea.Model, tea.Cmd) {
    switch msg := msg.(type) {
    case tickMsg:
        return m, pollContainers()
    case containersMsg:
        m.containers = msg
        return m, nil
    }
    return m, nil
}
```

## Layout

Split the viewport into panels and let each own its state. The parent model only routes messages downward and collects commands upward.

## Defaults matter

Run bare `wc` and you get three numbers. Any reimplementation that prints something else on zero flags has already lost the user's trust.

```go
var showLines = flag.Bool("l", false, "count lines")
var showWords = flag.Bool("w", false, "count words")
var showBytes = flag.Bool("c", false, "count bytes")
```

## One flag, one job

`-l`, `-w`, `-c` each do exactly one thing and compose cleanly. Combined flags fall out of the design instead of needing special cases.

## Handlers

Each route is a function with `(w, r)` and no magic. Routing, decoding, and responding are all visible in one place.

```go
mux.HandleFunc("GET /weather", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    city := r.URL.Query().Get("city")
    if city == "" {
        http.Error(w, "missing city", http.StatusBadRequest)
        return
    }
    // fetch, decode, respond...
})
```

## Errors

Return early, log once, and keep status codes honest. A handler that can't fail loudly will fail silently in production.
